PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
- Consensus: The deliberation maintains complete unanimity across all 10 colleagues for Choice B (increase, more, Keap1).
- Core Mechanism: Exposure of RAW 264.7 cells to the lipid electrophile (2E)-4-hydroxy-2-nonen-8-ynal activates Keap1-Nrf2 signaling via alkylation of reactive cysteines on Keap1, upregulating downstream Phase II enzymes including ALDH.
- Potency Comparison: Treatment with 50 µM 4-octyl itaconate (4-OI) induces a more robust accumulation of ALDH than the reactive alkenal due to its greater metabolic stability and lack of translational suppression at this concentration.
- Conclusion: Firmly aligned on B. increase, more, Keap1.
}

